verything at the  same time  well i do not know about you but for me  when i'm reading out loud i have to peek  much further away into the sequence to  get the right intonation  i also peek into the past by short-term  memorization which is  honestly better than the memory of the  lstm we are also  comfortable with cnns that do a very  similar kind of processing  as what happens in our eyes but applying  this kind of  biological inspiration on neural  networks and  restricting their solution space with  biases is also  unfair to them it is important to take  as much inspiration from the biological  optimization algorithm  especially when it comes to energy  efficiency but we should also look  beyond  like with the transformer that is free  of the lstm and cnn  inductive biases okay so we have seen  that images and text can be represented  the same and the transformer heavily  takes advantage of this but  if images and text are represented  equally does that  make them equal well no images and texts  are not  born equal therefore they become very  different  content-wise vision is often an  evolutionary necessity  and evolved independently in many  species language human stroke of genius  came  later after humans already had their  vision  being invented it is based upon many  agreed upon rules therefore text is in a  way  already compressed it omits a lot of  stuff  we do not say that the grass is green if  it is  a visual common knowledge also in the  data sets transformers are usually  trained on  there is little if no figurative speech  and there are very few ways to say  for example cat in english language in  contrast  images of cats can look very very  different from each  angle camera setting and so on so  omission of the visual information in  language the  necessary and somewhat arbitrary rules  of language  cultures and languages are few of the  many  things that make text and images quite  different in content  there is overlap but they are also  complementary  and miss coffee bean thinks that this is  so exciting that the representations and  the processing is similar but the  content and nature of text and images  are so different when the transformer  acts on language we sometimes do not see  the language phenomena because of so  much transformer complexity  and the other times we do not see the  transformer because of so much  language so it's more than helpful to  learn something about transformers  text and images by seeing them interact  in different constellations  it's more or less like a complicated  ablation study  and the takeaway for multimodal  integration of all these  three together is that even if text and  images  are represented the same they are not  equal  in their content giving transformer  models a hard time to integrate them  the tendency is to kind of ignore the  visual information and rely  on the text because text is already so  compressed  and its biases are more easy to be  picked up  to find out more about this vision and  language inequality and its effects on  multimodal learning check out our video  about this link below  in any case miss coffee bean gets out of  here now  right hey miss coffee bean miss coffee  bean miss coffee bean come  okay bye  [Music]  you

